WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.533 --> 00:00:02.302
In 2012, Zongchang Yu

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:05.105
left his job as an engineer at a company called ASML...

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:07.640
the only company in the world that can make this machine.

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:10.710
This machine makes the most 
advanced semiconductor chips

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.412
or microchips in the world.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:14.948
After he left ASML

00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:16.349
he started two new companies

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:18.651
one in the US and 
one in China. 

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:21.121
US and ASML lawyers 
would later allege that

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:24.257
Yu recruited other ASML engineers 
to his US company...

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:25.458
that they brought with them

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:27.627
stolen information about AMSL's machine...

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:30.030
and that it was all backed by the Chinese government.

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:32.632
This story is just one small piece of China's

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:35.435
monumental effort to transform one of the world's most global

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:37.971
and significant industries: 
semiconductors.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:41.374
But China's effort has increasingly locked it

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.343
in a struggle with the United States.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:44.611
This isn't about market share.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:45.612
This isn't about tariffs.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:47.080
This is about security.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.549
So how exactly did China and the US

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:52.218
enter into a Cold War over computer chips?

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:55.522
[pensive, driving electronic music]

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.896
[music fades]

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.298
This is the first semiconductor chip

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.867
invented in the 1950s by engineers in the US.

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:10.937
It's a piece of silicon with four transistors on it.

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:13.540
The more transistors, the more powerful the chip.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.275
By 1960

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:18.044
engineers had already made one 
with four times the transistors...

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:20.580
and each year they figured out ways to add more.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.016
So since the early 1960s, semiconductors

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:25.552
have improved at an exponential rate.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:27.821
This is Chris Miller, 
author of Chip War.

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:29.956
The founder of Intel, Gordon Moore

00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:33.426
predicted in 1965 that the computing power

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:36.496
produced by a single chip would double every year or so

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:39.666
and that rate has held true roughly up to the present.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:44.571
The first companies dedicated 
to making chips were in the US

00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:47.040
where they really just had one main customer:

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.341
the US government.

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:51.611
The first use cases were actually in guidance computers

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:53.947
that were in NASA's spacecraft

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.548
as well as in the missile system.

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:58.451
"The complex guidance equipment..."

00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:00.954
"The system's electronic brain..."

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:05.024
Since chip companies were making 
better and better chips each year

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:07.260
the US government believed 
a deep partnership with them

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.396
would ensure that it always had access 
to the most advanced ones.

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:14.334
The US government has believed that
computing has been a core

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:17.203
determinant of nations' power on the world stage.

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:19.772
You think about the ways that computing

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:22.242
has been used to crack codes in World War II

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:24.611
or to track Soviet submarines during the Cold War.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.112
I think that judgment is correct.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.115
At first, these chip companies handled 
the entire supply chain.

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:32.018
They designed the chips, manufactured them, and assembled them

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:34.320
into a package for installation into a product.

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.356
All within the US.

00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:38.525
But by the late 1960s, they realized

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:39.926
they could make a lot more money

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:41.828
designing chips for civilian products

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:43.163
like corporate computers.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.498
They just had to make 
a lot more of them

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:48.067
and a lot less expensively.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:51.304
So many chip companies moved 
their manufacturing and assembly

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:54.374
to factories in Japan, Taiwan,
South Korea and Hong Kong

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:55.708
where labor was cheaper.

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.710
And the US government 
encouraged them.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.378
All these countries were US allies or partners

00:02:59.378 --> 00:03:03.245
and this was a way to support their economies and deepen ties.

00:03:03.245 --> 00:03:05.000
At the same time, it banned these chip companies

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.902
from sharing technology with its rivals:

00:03:06.902 --> 00:03:08.368
the Soviet Union and China.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:12.659
It was a way to keep them years behind 
the US as chips advanced.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:16.296
Because of Moore's Law, 
advanced chips are

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:19.232
substantially better than previous generations.

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:21.868
If you're 5 years behind or 10 years behind

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.103
you're actually quite far behind the cutting edge.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.005
But it wasn't long before these allied governments

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:28.208
began investing in their own chip companies.

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:29.809
In the 1970s and 80s

00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:32.445
Toshiba in Japan and Samsung 
in South Korea

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.247
began designing and manufacturing chips

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:35.815
that rivaled the Americans'.

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:39.118
In the 1990s, a Taiwanese company, TSMC

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.920
got so good at manufacturing chips

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.556
that many companies in the US 
stopped doing it.

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.858
It meant that U.S. companies
were not the only ones

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.961
who could make the most advanced chips anymore.

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.396
And every country's chip industry

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:53.666
was increasingly reliant on other countries for the materials,

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:56.636
software, and equipment needed to make more complex chips.

00:03:57.971 --> 00:04:00.106
But while the US and its allies 
were pushing the limits

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:01.007
of chip technology...

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.209
China was lagging behind.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.445
In addition to the US blocking it 
from accessing chips

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:06.512
during the Cold War

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:08.848
many of China's brightest 
scientists and engineers

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:10.083
had been driven out of the country 

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:13.052
by the dictator Mao Zedong 
during the 60s and 70s.

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:15.188
But over the next few decades, new Chinese leaders

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:16.623
pushed to catch up.

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:18.725
By the 1990s, the Cold War was over.

00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:21.160
The US had become friendlier with China

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.162
and it had lifted most of its export controls.

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.531
And so China enticed many chip companies

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:27.600
to move their assembly operations to China.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:31.170
And by the 2000s, China dominated 
this end of the supply chain.

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:33.373
But China was importing more and more chips 

00:04:33.373 --> 00:04:35.008
to feed its assembly industry

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:36.776
and it put them in a tricky position.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.846
The Chinese government had studied the tech supply chain

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:42.382
and they realized the entire Chinese tech ecosystem

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:45.018
relied on a foundation of imported silicon

00:04:45.018 --> 00:04:46.786
from China's geopolitical adversaries

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:49.322
and the United States, from Japan, from Taiwan.

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:50.590
And China's leaders concluded

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.158
quite understandably, that this was a risk

00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:54.427
they were unwilling to continue to take.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.062
So the Chinese government poured money

00:04:56.062 --> 00:04:58.531
into its own chip design and manufacturing companies...

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:01.401
which increasingly partnered with non-Chinese firms.

00:05:02.068 --> 00:05:04.470
All on the hopes of creating a chip supply chain

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.939
that existed entirely within China.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.876
Soon, China could design, manufacture and assemble

00:05:09.876 --> 00:05:12.578
some older generations of chips, mostly on its own.

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:16.549
But it was still years away from making 
the most cutting edge chips.

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:20.620
This is one of those chips.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.456
It's got around 114 billion transistors on it.

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:26.159
Remember in 1960, 
chips had 4.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:28.294
Computing capabilities in the future

00:05:28.294 --> 00:05:30.530
just like the capabilities of the past

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:33.032
will be deployed to military uses.

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.101
The problem is, only a few companies in the world

00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:38.037
are involved in making them
and none are in China.

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:40.807
To start, only 3 American companies

00:05:40.807 --> 00:05:43.176
make the software needed to design advanced chips.

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:46.079
Then turning those designs into real chips

00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:49.582
requires a machine that's only made by one company: ASML.

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:53.319
But this machine requires equipment 
that's only made in the US.

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:55.988
Finally, only companies in 
Taiwan and South Korea

00:05:55.988 --> 00:06:00.026
can put it all together and manufacture 
the most advanced processor chips.

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:02.762
These companies are chokepoints in the supply chain...

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:05.698
and China was totally reliant on them for advanced chips.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.103
In 2019, police in the US went 
to arrest Zongchang Yu

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:11.170
but couldn't find him.

00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:15.541
Until he appeared later in China 
as the CEO of his company

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.177
that successfully made software like ASML's.

00:06:18.745 --> 00:06:20.380
Thanks to help from the Chinese government

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.248
his company was flourishing.

00:06:22.248 --> 00:06:24.250
And his story was just one of several instances

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.219
of IP theft in the chip industry.

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:27.954
The Chinese government has been

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:29.956
at the very least, passively supportive

00:06:29.956 --> 00:06:33.893
but in some cases, actively 
supportive of IP theft

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:35.361
because the Chinese government realizes

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:39.399
is that its companies are in a position of relative weakness.

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:42.869
In order to eventually decrease its reliance 
on this foreign supply chain

00:06:42.869 --> 00:06:46.472
China was identifying choke points like ASML and copying them.

00:06:47.974 --> 00:06:49.776
But the plan backfired.

00:06:49.776 --> 00:06:51.010
And this has really angered

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:52.345
the US government, other governments,

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:55.581
and caused them to take China's

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:57.950
subsidies as a more security focused issue

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.452
rather than just an economic issue.

00:06:59.452 --> 00:07:00.753
This was happening at the same time

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:02.722
that the relationship between the US and China

00:07:02.722 --> 00:07:04.891
was becoming less cordial and more competitive.

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:07.093
"China's market distortions

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:09.295
and the way they deal cannot be tolerated..."

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:13.366
"...a tremendous intellectual property theft situation..."

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:13.900
"...game on here..."

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.002
"...a trade war between the United States and China..."

00:07:16.035 --> 00:07:17.637
"Trump says he plans to impose 

00:07:17.637 --> 00:07:20.206
a 10% tariff increase on China..."

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:21.641
"...if they don't want to trade with us any more

00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:23.342
that would be fine with me."

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:26.078
In 2018, the Trump administration banned US companies

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:29.282
from selling components to ZTE, 
a Chinese tech company.

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:30.650
Then in 2019

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.852
it banned US companies from 
doing business with China's

00:07:32.852 --> 00:07:35.288
biggest tech company: Huawei and its affiliates.

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:38.391
These bans nearly bankrupted ZTE

00:07:38.391 --> 00:07:40.460
and dealt a significant blow 
to Huawei.

00:07:40.693 --> 00:07:44.063
In 2022, the next president,
Joe Biden, targeted

00:07:44.063 --> 00:07:46.232
China's chip industry more broadly.

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:50.236
First, it banned all US companies from
selling advanced chips to China

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:52.171
but it also blocked Chinese design companies 

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:54.373
from using US made design software

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:56.576
and US made manufacturing equipment.

00:07:56.576 --> 00:08:00.513
Plus, it banned global companies who use 
US semiconductor technology

00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:02.782
from selling advanced chips to China as well.

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:05.117
The U.S was exploiting these choke points

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:07.487
to stop China's chip industry in its tracks.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:08.821
These export controls represented

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:12.158
a really clear shift away from the view

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:15.394
that ultimately trade and 
tax exchanges with China

00:08:15.394 --> 00:08:18.664
were fundamentally positive sum 
to a much more

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:22.134
zero sum view of the 
technological competition.

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:25.004
Next, the US passed a law that would 
invest billions of dollars

00:08:25.004 --> 00:08:27.139
into its own chip manufacturing companies...

00:08:27.507 --> 00:08:30.109
and finalized a deal with Taiwan's biggest manufacturer

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:33.179
TSMC, to build manufacturing plants in the US.

00:08:33.379 --> 00:08:36.382
All to enable the U.S. to keep racing ahead.

00:08:36.382 --> 00:08:39.318
China and the US have a pretty similar view 
of the political stakes

00:08:39.318 --> 00:08:41.354
when it comes to semiconductors.

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:42.655
That's why the US government

00:08:42.655 --> 00:08:45.558
has made it a priority 
to defend the US lead.

00:08:45.558 --> 00:08:49.228
[pensive electronic music]

00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:52.231
But this has also put extraordinary pressure
on another conflict

00:08:52.231 --> 00:08:53.799
between the two countries.

00:08:53.799 --> 00:08:57.670
Since 1949, China has viewed Taiwan as a breakaway province

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:00.540
and has vowed to reunite with it, even threatening invasion.

00:09:01.107 --> 00:09:02.975
The US has vowed to protect Taiwan.

00:09:02.975 --> 00:09:05.778
But Taiwan also happens to own 
the most important choke point

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:07.113
in the chip supply chain.

00:09:07.346 --> 00:09:10.683
Taiwanese companies manufacture 63% of all chips...

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.653
and about 92% of all advanced chips.

00:09:13.653 --> 00:09:15.388
With companies that are indispensable

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:17.523
 to both the US and Chinese chip industries

00:09:17.523 --> 00:09:19.759
Taiwan has built itself some protection.

00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:22.595
But the US export controls forced Taiwan's companies

00:09:22.595 --> 00:09:23.162
to make a choice:

00:09:23.462 --> 00:09:26.098
defy the US and keep selling to China...

00:09:26.098 --> 00:09:29.569
or comply and cut off China 
from some of its chips.

00:09:29.569 --> 00:09:31.771
So far, they've signaled they'll cut off China.

00:09:32.138 --> 00:09:34.340
But as China and the US feud over chips

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:36.809
more and more choices like these are going to be imposed

00:09:36.809 --> 00:09:38.945
on countries and companies around the world.

00:09:38.945 --> 00:09:42.048
Asking them to pick sides in what looks a lot like

00:09:42.048 --> 00:09:43.783
a new Cold War.

